{"files":[{"content":"#include <algorithm>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <utility>\n#include <vector>\n\nusing namespace std;\n\nstatic const int MOD = 1000000007;\n\nstatic int mod_pow(int base, int exponent) {\n    long long result = 1;\n    long long value = base;\n    while (exponent > 0) {\n        if (exponent & 1) result = result * value % MOD;\n        value = value * value % MOD;\n        exponent >>= 1;\n    }\n    return static_cast<int>(result);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n\n    int n, m;\n    if (!(cin >> n >> m)) return 0;\n\n    vector<pair<int, int>> obstacles;\n    obstacles.reserve(m);\n    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {\n        int y, x;\n        cin >> y >> x;\n        obstacles.emplace_back(y, x);\n    }\n\n    // Keep the original row-wise DP for small grids, where its simple scans\n    // avoid the sorting and modular-combinatorics setup costs.\n    if (n <= 2048) {\n        vector<vector<bool>> blocked(n, vector<bool>(n, false));\n        for (const auto& cell : obstacles)\n            blocked[cell.first][cell.second] = true;\n\n        vector<int> previous(n, 0), current(n, 0);\n        if (!blocked[0][0]) previous[0] = 1;\n        for (int x = 1; x < n; ++x)\n            if (!blocked[0][x]) previous[x] = previous[x - 1];\n\n        for (int y = 1; y < n; ++y) {\n            current[0] = blocked[y][0] ? 0 : previous[0];\n            for (int x = 1; x < n; ++x) {\n                current[x] = blocked[y][x] ? 0 : (previous[x] + current[x - 1]) % MOD;\n            }\n            previous.swap(current);\n        }\n        cout << previous[n - 1] << '\\n';\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    sort(obstacles.begin(), obstacles.end());\n    obstacles.erase(unique(obstacles.begin(), obstacles.end()), obstacles.end());\n\n    const pair<int, int> start{0, 0};\n    const pair<int, int> finish{n - 1, n - 1};\n    if (binary_search(obstacles.begin(), obstacles.end(), start) ||\n        binary_search(obstacles.begin(), obstacles.end(), finish)) {\n        cout << 0 << '\\n';\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    const int max_steps = 2 * (n - 1);\n    vector<int> factorial(max_steps + 1), inverse_factorial(max_steps + 1);\n    factorial[0] = 1;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= max_steps; ++i)\n        factorial[i] = static_cast<long long>(factorial[i - 1]) * i % MOD;\n    inverse_factorial[max_steps] = mod_pow(factorial[max_steps], MOD - 2);\n    for (int i = max_steps; i > 0; --i)\n        inverse_factorial[i - 1] = static_cast<long long>(inverse_factorial[i]) * i % MOD;\n\n    auto choose = [&](int total, int part) -> int {\n        if (part < 0 || part > total) return 0;\n        return static_cast<long long>(factorial[total]) * inverse_factorial[part] % MOD *\n               inverse_factorial[total - part] % MOD;\n    };\n\n    vector<pair<int, int>> points = obstacles;\n    points.push_back(finish);\n    sort(points.begin(), points.end());\n    points.erase(unique(points.begin(), points.end()), points.end());\n\n    vector<int> ways(points.size());\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < points.size(); ++i) {\n        const int y = points[i].first;\n        const int x = points[i].second;\n        long long count = choose(x + y, x);\n        for (size_t j = 0; j < i; ++j) {\n            const int py = points[j].first;\n            const int px = points[j].second;\n            if (py <= y && px <= x) {\n                const int paths_between = choose((y - py) + (x - px), y - py);\n                count -= static_cast<long long>(ways[j]) * paths_between % MOD;\n                if (count < 0) count += MOD;\n            }\n        }\n        ways[i] = static_cast<int>(count);\n        if (points[i] == finish) {\n            cout << ways[i] << '\\n';\n            return 0;\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n","path":"src/solution.cpp"}],"hypothesis":"A hybrid retains the measured baseline-style row DP for small grids, where sparse sorting and factorial setup were observed to cost more, while using sparse obstacle inclusion-exclusion for large grids to avoid quadratic grid storage and traversal. The crossover is heuristic and controller measurement determines whether it helps.","notes":"The archived candidates share the same sparse-coordinate, factorial, and inclusion-exclusion approach; this proposal applies those declared ideas only above a size threshold and preserves a compact rolling-row DP below it. No commands or measurements were run.","research":{"queries":[],"urls":[]},"sources_kept":["9950c59b3cb4425e","940c2026786b4026","e7450fd422694f1c","4355f61debbf4fd1"],"transformations":["Use rolling-row dynamic programming with a dense blocked grid for n <= 2048, avoiding sparse preprocessing overhead on small grids.","For larger n, sort and deduplicate obstacle coordinates, explicitly reject blocked endpoints, and compute the destination count by sparse inclusion-exclusion.","Precompute factorial and inverse-factorial tables modulo 1,000,000,007 for constant-time binomial path counts."]}